            <ArticleTitle>Stabilisation of the wrist in rheumatoid disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>288-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Internal splintage was undertaken of fifteen wrists for pain and instability, using an intramedullary Rush nail. The patients were examined at an average of thirty-three months after operation. Spontaneous fusion in a useful position had occurred in all but one. The operation is simple and external immobilisation is unnecessary.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
